From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] a product can replace the font configuration, not only add to it

This build renders Chinese and English. AOSP's fonts.xml declares 116 font
families, 101 of them fallback families for scripts that have no locale, no
keyboard and no content here, and Typeface.init() runs in a static class
initializer with android.graphics.Typeface in preloaded-classes: the zygote
walks every family in that file on every boot, mmapping the font, calling
addFontFromBuffer and then FontFamily.freeze(), which reads the font's cmap and
builds its coverage bitmap. Those 101 families are also 136 font files, 8.43 MB.

Deleting them means installing a different fonts.xml, and there is no way for a
product to do that. The two mechanisms that exist do not apply:
LOCAL_OVERRIDES_PACKAGES is read only under ifeq ($(LOCAL_MODULE_CLASS),APPS)
in build/make/core/prebuilt_internal.mk, and LOCAL_OVERRIDES_MODULES only for
EXECUTABLES, with a pretty-error for every other class in base_rules.mk. A
second ETC module installing /system/etc/fonts.xml does not collide either,
which is worse than failing: kati merges the two copy rules for that one output
into a single build statement carrying both intermediates as prerequisites and
keeps the first recipe, so the file that lands is AOSP's and the build says
nothing. That is how this port's first attempt shipped AOSP's 116-family
configuration beside 37 of its 173 fonts -- a configuration naming 136 files
that were not there.

So the hook goes where the file is chosen, beside ADDITIONAL_FONTS_FILE, which
already lets a product add families to AOSP's file. One module keeps the path.

The two do not compose, and this refuses them together rather than letting one
win quietly: add_additional_fonts.py opens 'frameworks/base/data/fonts/fonts.xml'
by that name, taking only an output path and the additions as arguments, so a
replacement set alongside it would be a prerequisite whose content is never
read. Making them compose means giving that script the base file to read, which
is a second change for a combination no product in this tree asks for.

Nothing changes for a product that sets neither, which is every product in the
tree but this one.

--- a/data/fonts/Android.mk
+++ b/data/fonts/Android.mk
@@ -83,6 +83,8 @@ font_src_files :=
 
 ################################
 # Copies the font configuration file into system/etc for the product as fonts.xml.
+# In the case where $(REPLACEMENT_FONTS_FILE) is defined, that file is installed
+# in place of the $(AOSP_FONTS_FILE). The two cannot be combined; see below.
 # In the case where $(ADDITIONAL_FONTS_FILE) is defined, the content of $(ADDITIONAL_FONTS_FILE)
 # is added to the $(AOSP_FONTS_FILE).
 include $(CLEAR_VARS)
@@ -92,6 +94,27 @@ LOCAL_MODULE_CLASS := ETC
 
 AOSP_FONTS_FILE := frameworks/base/data/fonts/fonts.xml
 
+# A product that renders a subset of the world's scripts installs its own
+# configuration here rather than through a module of its own: one module has to
+# keep /system/etc/fonts.xml. A second module installing that path does not
+# replace this one and does not collide with it either -- the two copy rules
+# become one rule carrying both prerequisites, with the first recipe, which is
+# this one. Nor can such a module declare the replacement:
+# LOCAL_OVERRIDES_PACKAGES is read only under ifeq ($(LOCAL_MODULE_CLASS),APPS)
+# in prebuilt_internal.mk, and LOCAL_OVERRIDES_MODULES only for EXECUTABLES.
+#
+# Not combinable with ADDITIONAL_FONTS_FILE: add_additional_fonts.py opens
+# 'frameworks/base/data/fonts/fonts.xml' by that name and takes only the output
+# path and the additions as arguments, so the replacement would be a dependency
+# whose content is never read. Refusing is the only honest answer here; making
+# them compose means giving that script the base file to read.
+ifdef REPLACEMENT_FONTS_FILE
+ifdef ADDITIONAL_FONTS_FILE
+$(error REPLACEMENT_FONTS_FILE and ADDITIONAL_FONTS_FILE cannot both be set)
+endif
+AOSP_FONTS_FILE := $(REPLACEMENT_FONTS_FILE)
+endif
+
 ifdef ADDITIONAL_FONTS_FILE
 ADDITIONAL_FONTS_SCRIPT := frameworks/base/tools/fonts/add_additional_fonts.py
 ADD_ADDITIONAL_FONTS := $(local-generated-sources-dir)/fonts.xml
